---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import RootLayout from "../../layouts/RootLayout.astro";
import ProgramPage from "../../layouts/ProgramPage.astro";
import { sideBarConstants, sideBarForPrograms } from "../../constants";

const chemicalPosts = await getCollection("programPost", ({ data }) => {
  return data.programArea.includes("Chemical");
});

const pageTitle = "Chemical Management";

const chemicalAreas = [
  {
    title: "Regulations, Guidance, and Policy",
    children: [
      { title: "Executive Orders" },
      { title: "Laws, Regulations, and Agreements" },
      { title: "Guidance" },
    ],
  },

  {
    title: "Supporting Information and Tools",
    children: [
      { title: "Databases and Software Tools" },
      { title: "Directories, Catalogs, and Newsletters" },
      { title: "Libraries and Repositories" },
      { title: "Organizations and Programs" },
    ],
  },

  {
    title: "Lessons Learned",
    children: [
      { title: "Award Winners" },
      { title: "Accident Prevention and Reporting" },
      { title: "Case Studies" },
      { title: "Chemical Identification" },
      { title: "Chemical Use Reduction" },
      { title: "Community Right-to-Know" },
    ],
  },

  { title: "Training, Presentations, and Briefings" },
  { title: "Conferences and Events" },
];
---

<ProgramPage
  programAreas={chemicalAreas}
  posts={chemicalPosts}
  pageTitle={pageTitle}
>
  <p>
    The focus of the Chemical Management Program Area is emergency planning,
    community right-to-know, and reporting of substances and processes at
    facilities which may pose a threat. This program area does not solely apply
    to manufacturers or large federal facilities. Chemicals include liquids,
    solids, and gaseous substances. A substance as common as "white-out" or a
    household pesticide/herbicide are considered a chemical as well as paints,
    strippers, thinners, toluene, mercury, chlorine, and trichloroethane.
  </p>
  <p>
    When future Executive Orders (EOs) and Implementing Instructions for those
    EOs are released addressing federal facilities the text on this page will be
    updated.
  </p>
  <p>
    The CEQ Memo states, "Each agency must continue to comply with the
    provisions set forth in sections 301 through 313 of EPCRA, as amended, in
    light of applicable guidance issued by EPA, and without regard to the
    Standard Industrial Classification (SIC) or North American Industrial
    Classification System (NAICS) delineations."
  </p>
  <!-- TODO: Add the rest of the chemical stuff, can it be shortened? -->
</ProgramPage>
